  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 347 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will remain anchored near the Four Corners \nregion yet Tuesday. West to northwest flow aloft will be in place \nacross our area on the northeast periphery of the high. Expect one \nmore hot day with highs well in the 90s at most locations. \n\nThe upper level ridging flattens some with the center translating \ninto the southern plains my the middle and latter parts of the week. \nEnsemble guidance suggests somewhat stronger winds aloft will \ndevelop southward from the northern plains as the ridge flattens. At \nthe same time, mean PWAT values will be on the increase, as \nmonsoonal/subtropical moisture aloft flows northward across the \ndesert southwest and turns eastward across the region on the \nnorthern edge of the southern plains upper high. The ENS suggests \nmean PWAT values greater than 1.5\" by Wednesday night and lasting \nthrough at least Friday. Several embedded shortwaves are expected \nwithin the westerly flow. The result will be increasing chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms Wednesday and beyond. Will have to monitor \nthe potential for some localized heavy rainfall amounts with such an \nabundance of atmospheric moisture.   \n\nA considerable cool down also looks to be on tap by the of the week. \nAt the surface, A Canadian airmass attempts to nose southward into \nthe northern and central plains. Current forecast guidance suggests \nseveral days of highs in the lower to mid 70s Thursday into next \nweekend.",
